5,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,2629,315,279,9904,311,198,78045,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,59,37018,90,18,15170,17,92,1124,15940,220,17,87,59,60,258,279,9873,400,15,1124,273,856,1124,273,220,17,1124,2493,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of the solutions to\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\]in the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2 \\pi.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d50fb52595b342df7bd590fe9bef5cf82357be1e6cb2f0131665e44e20b85987"},"seconds":52.574723958969116,"seed":352640535286952758,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x.\\]\n\nFirst, we use the double-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\), to rewrite the equation:\n\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\n\nNext, we factor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\n\nWe can rearrange this equation to:\n\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0.\\]\n\nThis gives us two cases to consider:\n\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\)\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\)\n\nFor the first case, \\(\\sin x = 0\\), the solutions in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are:\n\n\\[x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi.\\]\n\nFor the second case, we need to solve the equation \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\). Using the identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\), we substitute and get:\n\n\\[2(1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0.\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0,\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0,\\]\n\\[2 \\cos^2 x - 3 \\cos x + 1 = 0.\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in terms of \\(\\cos x\\). We solve it using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\cos x\\):\n\n\\[2y^2 - 3y + 1 = 0.\\]\n\nThe quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\) gives:\n\n\\[y = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}.\\]\n\nSo, the solutions are:\n\n\\[y = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\n\nThis means \\(\\cos x = 1\\) or \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\). For \\(\\cos x = 1\\), the solution in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) is:\n\n\\[x = 0.\\]\n\nFor \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), the solutions in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are:\n\n\\[x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}.\\]\n\nCombining all the solutions, we have:\n\n\\[x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}.\\]\n\nThe sum of these solutions is:\n\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 5\\pi.\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the solutions is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{5\\pi}.\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5\\pi}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
